const code = `use std::sync::Arc;
use stateknot::agent_http::{AgentHttpOptions, AgentHttpService};

// service: AgentServiceV1 with exact deployments and resource policy.
// verifier: your real AgentHttpAuthenticator, not a test token check.
let options = AgentHttpOptions::new(["agents.example.com".to_owned()])?;
let http = AgentHttpService::new(service, Arc::new(verifier), options);
let router = http.router();
// Mount behind TLS. Run Workers separately. Own and drain the listener.`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Submit and recover Agents over authenticated HTTP."
  description="Four bounded JSON operations over the durable Agent service, with verified identity, exact retries and two-phase cancellation."
  section="Agent HTTP v1"
  locale="en"
>
  <aside
    class="docs-callout docs-callout--boundary"
    aria-labelledby="http-boundary"
  >
    <h2 id="http-boundary">Implemented JSON profile, not a stable release</h2>
    <p>
      <code>AgentHttpService</code> adds a real HTTP router over <a
        href="/docs/agent-service/">AgentService v1</a
      >. It durably admits work but never executes a model, Tool or graph in the
      request task. StateKnot remains pre-alpha. Activity SSE is now opt-in;
      complete managed hosting and stable release qualification remain separate
      work.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-wiring">
    <h2 id="http-wiring">Bind verified identity and resource policy</h2>
    <p>
      A mandatory <code>AgentHttpAuthenticator</code> validates signature or introspection
      evidence, issuer, audience, expiry and revocation. It maps a trusted tenant/principal
      and independent <code>Submit</code>, <code>Read</code> and <code
        >Cancel</code
      > grants. There is no default verifier or anonymous mode.
    </p>
    <p>
      The existing <code>AgentServiceAuthorizer</code> still authorizes the exact
      Agent/request, Run or key digest before resource lookup. Never trust identity
      fields in JSON, an unverified JWT or arbitrary forwarding headers. Client budgets
      can only narrow host policy.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · host wiring</span><button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="http-code"
          ><span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span></button
        >
      </div>
      <pre id="http-code" tabindex="0"><code>{code}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-identity">
    <h2 id="http-identity">
      Connect a real identity provider with online verification
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>AgentHttpIntrospection</code> connects to a fixed HTTPS OAuth 2.0 introspection
      endpoint and strictly verifies issuer, audience, subject, lifetime and operation
      scopes. Real Keycloak 26.7.3 TLS, secret rotation and revocation are qualified.
      No redirects, environment proxies, retries, token cache or insecure TLS switch.
      This is not JWT/JWKS local validation or complete OIDC.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>TenantPolicy</code> accepts exact principal-to-tenant bindings from the
      trusted control plane, intersecting token scopes with local grants. At most
      1024 bindings have an explicit lease of at most one hour;
      <code>replace</code> atomically checks the expected generation. Expired policy
      fails closed. A tenant binding is not Run ownership: the existing
      <code>AgentServiceAuthorizer</code> must still authorize each resource.
    </p>
    <p>
      Install secret-manager-delivered credentials with
      <code>replace_client_secret</code>. Readiness sends a random invalid token
      using real client authentication and requires an inactive response. It
      proves endpoint/credential access, not business authorization or resource
      policy health. Rotation without overlapping credentials creates a brief
      503 window; never fall back to anonymous access.
    </p>
    <p>
      Defaults are a 3-second total exchange, 32 concurrent requests and a 64
      KiB response. Every HTTP request and SSE authorization cycle verifies
      online; plan IdP capacity accordingly. Buffered bytes cannot be revoked.
      Test identity services and accounts are never deployed with the website.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/agent-identity.md"
        >Complete wiring, rotation and recovery guide</a
      >
      · <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0011-agent-http-introspection.md"
        >RFC-0011 (Draft)</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-worker">
    <h2 id="http-worker">Run execution separately</h2>
    <p>
      The owned <a href="/docs/agent-loop/#owned-worker"
        >durable scheduling Worker</a
      >
      now manages concrete tenant/fair schedulers, real dependency readiness, fixed
      concurrency and joined shutdown. Ingress remains independent; submitting a Run
      does not start a Worker or maintenance jobs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Run the <a href="/docs/agent-loop/#owned-maintenance"
        >owned maintenance role</a
      >
      separately for deadline cancellation, child settlement, Join publication and
      failure closure, including when no execution Run is runnable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="http-resource-policy">
    <h2 id="http-resource-policy">
      Real resource policy: exact grants and recoverable submission
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>runtime::agent_policy::AgentResourcePolicy</code> provides offline default-deny
      authorization. Submission rules match tenant, issuer/subject, Agent revision
      and input schema, with explicit scopes and restrictive budgets. Run reads, cancellation
      and key lookup are separate permissions. Knowing a Run ID is not permission;
      <code>TenantRuns</code>
      is an explicitly privileged operator grant, never inferred ownership.
    </p>
    <p>
      Build a <code>PolicyArtifact</code> from a <code>PolicyDocument</code>,
      retaining its canonical bytes privately with a trusted digest before
      activation. Limits are 1024 total rules and 256 KiB JSON; duplicate or
      overlapping selectors fail closed. Call
      <code>register_agent_policy_evidence_schema</code> before freezing the registry,
      then pass the shared policy to <code>AgentServiceV1</code> and compose resource
      and identity readiness.
    </p>
    <p>
      Absolute expiration survives restart, and the local freshness lease is at
      most one hour.
      <code>replace</code> uses expected-generation CAS. Expiration returns 503; no
      matching rule returns 403. SSE closes on subsequent authorization after revocation.
      Admission retains selected-rule, request and budget evidence; unrelated ACL
      changes preserve same-key recovery. If changing the applicable submission rule
      produces 409, recover through a currently authorized key lookup instead of automatically
      resubmitting under a new key.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reads and denials are not automatically durably audited; cancellation
      retains policy and decision hashes only. Hosts must retain protected audit
      context as required. Real PostgreSQL 16/17 and Keycloak tests use this
      implementation; automatic ownership storage and full production-release
      acceptance remain separate.
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/agent-resource-policy.md"
        >Configuration, wiring and operations guide</a
      >
      · <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0012-agent-resource-policy.md"
        >RFC-0012 (Draft)</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-operations">
    <h2 id="http-operations">Four operations, one durable service</h2>
    <dl class="docs-definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>POST /v1/agent-runs</dt><dd>
          Serialize <code>AgentHttpSubmission</code>: submission_key, exact
          versioned agent identity, and schema-bound request. 201 creates a Run;
          200 recovers it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>GET /v1/agent-runs/&#123;run_id&#125;</dt><dd>
          No request body. 200 returns an integrity-verified public snapshot,
          not private checkpoints or transcript.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>POST /v1/agent-runs/lookup</dt><dd>
          Serialize <code>AgentHttpLookup</code> with submission_key. Keep the key
          out of the URL and access logs.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>POST /v1/agent-runs/&#123;run_id&#125;/cancellation</dt><dd>
          Serialize caller-retained <code>AgentCancellationIds</code> with event_id
          and failure_id. 202 records the request; 200 recovers the exact pair.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Use a Bearer credential and JSON POST bodies. Accept must be absent, <code
        >application/json</code
      > or <code>*/*</code>. Rust wire types expose JSON Schema; unknown fields,
      duplicate keys (including nested keys), query strings and noncanonical
      paths are rejected.
    </p>
    <p>
      Success returns <code
        >AgentHttpRunResponse &#123; request_id, snapshot &#125;</code
      >. A fresh request_id correlates this response; it is not an idempotency
      key. <code>Active</code> does not prove a Worker is executing and may include
      failure closure. <code>CancellationRequested</code> is not terminal cleanup.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-sse">
    <h2 id="http-sse">Replay activity after a disconnect</h2>
    <p>
      Enable <code>with_sse(AgentHttpSseOptions::default())</code> on the same authenticated
      host. Connect to <code>GET /v1/agent-runs/&#123;run_id&#125;/events</code>
      with Bearer authentication and <code>Accept: text/event-stream</code>.
      Reconnect with the last completely processed <code>Last-Event-ID</code>.
      Native EventSource cannot set this Authorization header; use an
      authenticated streaming HTTP client. No credentials in URLs or anonymous
      fallback.
    </p>
    <dl class="docs-definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>activity</dt><dd>
          One public notification per retained journal entry. Its id is a
          database-verified replay cursor; JSON contains only a decimal-string
          sequence and recording time. No private payload, custom kind or
          transcript.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>snapshot</dt><dd>
          A current verified AgentHttpRunResponse, without an id. It does not
          describe historical state at a cursor. Intermediate snapshots may
          coalesce; quarantine-only changes are still detected.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>error / heartbeat</dt><dd>
          Neither advances replay progress. Errors are best-effort, heartbeats
          are comments, and EOF never proves task success.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Process an activity idempotently before saving its cursor. A new process
      resumes strictly after that exact retained event. Malformed cursors are
      400; changed, crossed or unavailable history returns <code
        >agent_http.invalid_cursor</code
      >
      (409). Never silently reset progress. This is not token streaming, complete
      historical state replay, or an exactly-once delivery guarantee.
    </p>
    <p>
      Defaults: 16 stream producers, 60-second lifetime, 1-second
      polling/heartbeats, 5-second queue wait, one queued bounded batch, 64 MiB
      cumulative output. Streams use separate capacity from JSON requests.
      Credentials and resource policy are rechecked each iteration; already
      authorized buffered bytes cannot be recalled. Drop, queue timeout and
      shutdown stop producer work. Configure socket limits and disable proxy
      buffering/compression on this route.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/agent-events.md"
        >Full client recovery and production-host guide</a
      >
      · <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0009-agent-sse-replay.md"
        >RFC-0009</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-server">
    <h2 id="http-server">Own readiness, connections and graceful drain</h2>
    <p>
      <code>AgentHttpServer::start</code> owns a loopback-only HTTP/1 listener behind
      a co-located TLS proxy. Startup verifies the actual PostgreSQL schema and exact
      executable registry, plus a mandatory host-provided
      <code>AgentHttpReadiness</code> check of the same credential-verifier and policy
      dependencies used by requests. No placeholder verifier, migration, Worker or
      scheduler starts implicitly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Single-flight checks repeat every 10 seconds after completion, with a
      5-second whole-check timeout and 20-second evidence freshness by default.
      Failed, timed-out or stale readiness rejects new business requests with
      503; a later successful check restores admission. This is not an
      authorization grant or a guarantee that a future request succeeds. Already
      admitted work retains its normal authorization and deadlines.
    </p>
    <p>
      Transport defaults are 256 connections, 64 headers, a 32 KiB parser
      buffer, 5-second header reads and a 300-second absolute connection
      lifetime. Excess sockets close without a business response. JSON/SSE
      limits remain separate. TLS, proxy limits and replica-wide quotas are
      still deployment responsibilities.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>server.health()</code> exposes only local status and active connection
      counts. Wire these to a protected admin or supervisor surface; there is no public
      health exception in the Agent API. Unavailable is live but not ready.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>server.begin_shutdown()</code> closes readiness immediately. Await
      <code>server.shutdown()</code> to stop accepting, finish admitted work, then
      abort and join remaining connection tasks after the default 30-second drain.
      SSE may require force-close. Inspect the drain report and preserve original
      retry identities: closure never proves rollback. Dropping the handle aborts
      work but does not await graceful drain. Blocking callbacks still require an
      outer process-supervisor kill deadline; shared DB pools remain host-owned.
    </p>
    <p>
      Real PostgreSQL 16/17 tests cover readiness loss/recovery, startup
      failure, commit during drain, slow-body/SSE force-close, idle/drop cleanup
      and transport ceilings. Real issuer/policy-backed role integration and
      stable release qualification remain separate gates.
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/agent-http-server.md"
        >Complete wiring and operations guide</a
      >
      · <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0010-owned-agent-http-server.md"
        >RFC-0010</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-retry">
    <h2 id="http-retry">Recover a lost response without creating more work</h2>
    <p>
      Persist a high-entropy submission key and exact logical request before
      sending. Persist both cancellation IDs before cancelling. On timeout,
      disconnect, shutdown or an oversized response, the database may already
      have committed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read by the original key/Run or retry the same content and identities with
      bounded backoff. Changed submission content or a competing cancellation
      pair is 409. Inspect durable state; do not generate a new key merely
      because the response was lost. A recovered snapshot may have a newer
      revision.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-limits">
    <h2 id="http-limits">Finite limits and payload-safe errors</h2>
    <p>
      Defaults: 256 KiB request, 2 MiB response, 64 concurrent requests per
      shared instance and a 15-second cooperative deadline. Hard ceilings: 2 MiB
      request, 8 MiB response, 1,024 concurrent requests and 60 seconds. JSON
      also has structural bounds. Blocking host code cannot be preempted by an
      async timeout.
    </p>
    <p>
      400 rejects malformed input; 401/403 reject credential or policy; 404
      follows authorization; 405/406/415 reject unsupported HTTP forms; 409
      reports conflict; 413 rejects body size/read failures; 429 signals
      capacity; 503 signals unavailable/deadline/shutdown; 500 signals
      integrity/configuration or response limits.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every response is private/no-store JSON with a correlation ID and
      API-version header. Errors expose only a fixed <code>agent_http.*</code> code
      and request_id, never raw token, SQL or provider messages. 429 and 503 include
      <code>Retry-After: 1</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-operations-checklist">
    <h2 id="http-operations-checklist">
      Before exposing a production endpoint
    </h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Pin compatible source, lockfile, migrations, graphs and schemas. Run an
        independently configured scheduler/Worker; submission alone does not
        advance a Run.
      </li>
      <li>
        Configure TLS and an exact Host allowlist, connection/header limits,
        per-tenant quotas and replica-wide admission control. Forwarded headers
        do not expand trust. Origin is denied by default; optional exact HTTPS
        origins do not enable CORS.
      </li>
      <li>
        Install real credential verification and exact resource policy. Exercise
        expiry, revocation, wrong audience and tenant isolation. Never deploy
        the tests’ static credentials.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep authorization, keys, input/output and raw paths out of logs.
        Monitor bounded latency/error metrics and dependency readiness.
      </li>
      <li>
        Await <code>server.shutdown()</code> when using the owned role. Router-only
        hosts still call <code>http.shutdown()</code> and bound their own connection
        drain. Recover possibly committed requests with original identities after
        replacement.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Qualification covers actual HTTP + PostgreSQL 16/17, 24 concurrent
      identical submissions, lost submit/cancel responses, no inline dispatch,
      hostile input, authorization before an unavailable database, slow
      requests, overload and shutdown. SSE additionally covers new-process
      replay, revoked access, quarantine-only changes and unpolled responses.
      This does not qualify a live issuer, live model or an entire production
      deployment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/agent-http.md"
        >Full wire and operations guide</a
      > · <a
